{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "The Good Old Days"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Dreaming Big"}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 8}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": " Identify and describe events in a range of social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": " Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations. "}], "type": "objectives"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Adjectives About Feelings and Reactions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You talk about feelings and reactions using adjectives.  It is important to use the correct kind of adjective - it is common for English language learners to confuse the two kinds of adjectives when talking about feelings and reactions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "1. -ed adjectives"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Adjectives that end in <span class=\"strong\">-ed </span> are used to describe how a person <span class=\"strong\">feels</span>, like <span class=\"italic\">annoyed </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> fascinated </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> entertained </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> surprised </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> worried </span> or <span class=\"italic\"> tired </span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">I am annoyed. <br/> Parents are thrilled.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "2. -ing adjectives"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Adjectives that end in <span class=\"strong\">-ing </span> are used to describe things and situations, like <span class=\"italic\">annoying </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> entertaining </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> surprising </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> worrying</span> or <span class=\"italic\">tiring</span>. These adjectives describe the <span class=\"strong\">effect</span> something has on a person."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">The study is compelling.</span> (It makes me feel compelled).<br/> <span class=\"italic\">Work is sometimes surprising, too.</span> (It makes me feel tired)."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Media publicity ensured entertaining events. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Watching sports captivated the audience more than playing them."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Used to and Would"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would</span> and <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> have similar meanings. They are both used to describe something that happened regularly in the past but doesn't happen any longer. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "However, there is an important difference between <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> and <span class=\"strong\">would</span>.<br><br>   <span class=\"strong\">Would</span> is not used unless you know that the time frame is in the past, while <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> does not require this. <br><br>  Read the following example: <br>  <span class=\"italic\">We would visit India every summer when we were children.</span><br>  When we were children refers to a past time frame and therefore makes the meaning of would clear.<br><br>  <span class=\"italic\">We used to visit India every summer. </span><br>  Here, you do not have to include a past time frame since the meaning of <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> clear."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Sports used to be largely amatuer events. "}, {"type": "example", "content": " People would play fewer sports as they began to watch them more on TV."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "sportsmanship"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " fair play, following the rules of a game and being a good loser "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The games no longer focus on sportsmanship."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use to talk about sports and interests."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "couch potato "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " a person who spends little or no time exercising and a great deal of time watching television "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: People spend more time watching sports than playing, a phenomenon known as being a couch potato."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "amateur "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a non-professional"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": " Example: Sports used to be largely amateur events."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to capitalize"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make money "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Sports became an opportunity to capitalize and make money."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to exploit "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make full use of a gain; benefits from someone or something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: A few are exploited to provide entertainment to many."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "moral "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a person's standard of behavior"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": " Example: Sports no longer focus on moral development."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn7le08ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "With a partner, discuss this question: Do you think people care more about the game or the athlete? Why?"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe your opinion using adjectives."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Keep the discussion going by asking follow-up questions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Try to come to a conclusion with your partner."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I discuss the issue of sports."}, {"type": "text", "content": "My